/* =====================================================================
 * XquiX Studio — player rig renderer
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Draws every player on the board from MIZE's painted artwork instead of
 * a flat PNG, so a player can hold joint angles.
 *
 * Dependency surface, mirroring xquix-game-tracker.js:
 *   1. this file, after the Studio scripts
 *   2. window.MIZE            — already exported by Studio
 *   3. window.playerIconSrc   — the Studio's own "what image should this
 *                               player show" choke point (index.html)
 * No Studio source changes are required.
 *
 * HOW IT HOOKS IN
 * `playerIconSrc(team,pose,label,category)` is wrapped, not replaced: the
 * original still handles every sport that is not water polo, so the abstract
 * marker fallback is untouched. For water polo it returns an SVG data URI of
 * the rig instead of a PNG path.
 *
 * The signature has no element in it, so it cannot see whose angles to use.
 * `sizePlayer(el)` is therefore wrapped as well, purely to note which element
 * is being drawn while the original runs. That is the only reason it exists —
 * it changes nothing about sizing.
 *
 * WHY A DATA URI AND NOT INLINE SVG
 * Everything downstream — ghosts, tutorial ghosts, the sizing maths, the ball's
 * hand offsets — expects an <img> with a src. Keeping that contract means this
 * module is one wrapped function rather than a rewrite of the render path.
 * Markup is cached by (icon key + angles), so a board of 26 players at rest
 * builds a handful of strings, exactly as cheap as twelve PNGs.
 *
 * ANGLES
 * `el.dataset.rigAngles` is an optional JSON object of joint angles in degrees
 * offset from rest, e.g. {"shoulder_R":46,"elbow_R":-58} — the pose format the
 * pose library exports. Absent or unparseable means rest, which is what the
 * PNG always showed, so a board that never sets it looks the same as before.
 * ===================================================================== */
